It accepts both but in most cases gray is primary and grey is processed
as an alternate spelling:

$ grep -i grey * | wc -l
14
$ grep -i gray * | wc -l
130

The exception at first glance appears to be grey elves:

$ grep GREY *
mondata.c:              { "gray-elf",           PM_GREY_ELF },
mondata.c:              { "grey elf",           PM_GREY_ELF },
mondata.c:              { "gray elf",           PM_GREY_ELF },
mondata.c:      {PM_GREEN_ELF, PM_ELF_LORD}, {PM_GREY_ELF,
PM_ELF_LORD},
$ grep GRAY_ELF *

           names[] = {
            /* Alternate spellings */
                { "grey dragon",        PM_GRAY_DRAGON },
                { "baby grey dragon",   PM_BABY_GRAY_DRAGON },
                { "grey unicorn",       PM_GRAY_UNICORN },
                { "grey ooze",          PM_GRAY_OOZE },
                { "gray-elf",           PM_GREY_ELF },

Various other parts of mondata.c show that the primary spelling
for dragons and ooze are gray, but the primary spelling for elf
is grey.



